Date: Sat, 6 Jun 2026 13:12:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/33] fix(verimark): USB-level reset at open() to recover
 from stuck state
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Mirrors python/verimark/match.py:reset_dev() which calls dev.reset()
followed by a 0.3 s sleep at the start of every operation. Without it,
the C driver inherits any kernel USB driver / sensor firmware state
left by a previous fprintd that was SIGKILL-ed mid-control-transfer —
new control transfers then queue without ever completing, and verify
hangs silently inside the polling loop (the kernel never returns
LIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT because the URB is still pending).

Runtime symptom on WULFENIX: after a verify or enroll was interrupted
(Ctrl+C, KDE settings hand-off, etc.), the next fprintd-verify keeps
the white LED on but the FRAME_READY event never reaches us, and the
30 s deadline never fires either — the entire driver thread is blocked
inside the first USB read of the first 0x87 event_read.

g_usb_device_reset is best-effort: failures (no permission, device
gone) are silently ignored and the downstream claim_interface call
reports the real error.

Drafted with AI assistance.
---
 libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c b/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c
index dc7c66c..c12ba77 100644
--- a/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c
+++ b/libfprint/drivers/verimark/verimark.c
@@ -266,6 +266,17 @@ static void verimark_open(FpDevice *device)
     /* Plain pointer — open_complete steals the GError reference. */
     GError *error = NULL;
 
+    /* USB-level reset before claiming. Mirrors python/verimark/match.py
+     * reset_dev() which does the same. Crucial for robustness across
+     * interrupted previous sessions : if a prior fprintd was SIGKILLed
+     * mid-USB-transfer, the kernel USB driver and/or sensor firmware
+     * can be left in a state where new control transfers queue without
+     * ever completing. A reset clears both ends. Best-effort — if the
+     * reset fails (no permission, device gone), continue and let the
+     * downstream claim_interface report the real error. */
+    g_usb_device_reset(usb, NULL);
+    g_usleep(300 * 1000);   /* 0.3s — same as python reset_dev() */
+
     if (!g_usb_device_claim_interface(usb, VERIMARK_MI_BIO, 0, &error)) {
         fpi_device_open_complete(device, error);
         return;
